{
  "id": "ep023",
  "duration": 52.615,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 4.043,
      "text": "Male deep-sea anglerfish dissolve their own bodies to survive.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Caulophryne pelagica.jpg",
        "credit": "D. Shale",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caulophryne_pelagica.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 4.033,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 4.043,
      "end": 10.913,
      "text": "In the pitch-black deep sea, finding a mate is nearly impossible for the two hundred known species of anglerfish.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.033,
          "end": 10.9,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 10.913,
      "end": 17.334,
      "text": "Females use bioluminescent lures to attract prey and can swallow animals twice their own size.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Melanocetus johnsoni.png",
        "credit": "D. ROSS ROBERTSON, ARTURO ANGULO, CAROLE C. BALDWIN, DIANE PITASSY, AMY DRISKELL, LEE WEIGT, IGNACIO J.F. NAVARRO",
        "license": "CC BY 3.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melanocetus_johnsoni.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 10.9,
          "end": 17.333,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 17.334,
      "end": 25.054,
      "text": "Moving down the ladder, male Photocorynus spiniceps are among the smallest vertebrates at about seven millimeters long.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 17.333,
          "end": 25.067,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 25.054,
      "end": 30.024,
      "text": "That makes the massive female up to sixty times larger than her tiny mate.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Representatives of ceratioid families.jpg",
        "credit": "Masaki Miya et al.",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Representatives_of_ceratioid_families.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 25.067,
          "end": 30.033,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "depth"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 30.024,
      "end": 36.319,
      "text": "When a male finally finds a female, he latches on with his teeth to begin a permanent transformation.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Representatives of ceratioid families.jpg",
        "credit": "Masaki Miya et al.",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Representatives_of_ceratioid_families.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 30.033,
          "end": 36.333,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "depth"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 36.319,
      "end": 42.738,
      "text": "His eyes and internal organs slowly wither away until only his reproductive organs remain.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Wissenschaftliche Ergebnisse der Deutschen Tiefsee-Expedition auf dem Dampfer \"Valdivia\" 1898-1899 (Tafel 15) (7413862480).jpg",
        "credit": "Chun, Carl",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wissenschaftliche_Ergebnisse_der_Deutschen_Tiefsee-Expedition_auf_dem_Dampfer_%22Valdivia%22_1898-1899_(Tafel_15)_(7413862480).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 36.333,
          "end": 42.733,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 42.738,
      "end": 49.283,
      "text": "He can fuse into her body because he lacks the immune genes that would normally reject foreign tissue.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "A manual of human physiology, including histology and microscopical anatomy, with special reference to the requirements of practical medicine (1885) (14758608866).jpg",
        "credit": "Internet Archive Book Images",
        "license": "No restrictions",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_manual_of_human_physiology,_including_histology_and_microscopical_anatomy,_with_special_reference_to_the_requirements_of_practical_medicine_(1885)_(14758608866).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 42.733,
          "end": 49.3,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 49.283,
      "end": 52.265,
      "text": "This dependence on her blood supply is why.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Caulophryne pelagica.jpg",
        "credit": "D. Shale",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caulophryne_pelagica.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 49.3,
          "end": 52.6,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
